<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: The Mongol World</h1><p class="ws-lede">Follow Genghis Khan's empire from the steppe to the rival khanates and Timur's heirs.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/the-mongol-world">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the stops from 1 (earliest) to 6 (latest), by when each began: a state&#8217;s founding, a person&#8217;s birth, the date of an event.</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Ilkhanate</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Mongol Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Golden Horde</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Yuan Dynasty</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Timurid Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Hulagu Khan</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Match the people to their dates</h2><p class="ws-how">Write the number of the right dates next to each person. Their pages on the path will help.</p><div class="ws-match"><ol class="ws-ma" type="A"><li>Batu Khan</li><li>Hulagu Khan</li><li>Kublai Khan</li></ol><ol class="ws-mb"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>c. 1205–1255</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>1215–1294</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>c. 1217–1265</li></ol></div></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Key terms</h2><p class="ws-how">The path uses these words. Explain each one in your own words.</p><ul class="ws-terms"><li><b>Inflation</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><b>Silk Road</b><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span></span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">4</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Secret History of the Mongols</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;The oldest surviving work of Mongolian literature tells of Temüjin's youth and his struggles with the Tatars and Merkits before his rise to power.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 6, <b>Ilkhanate</b>: &#8220;Hulagu's khanate in Persia and Iraq became a Persianised Islamic state under Ghazan, who converted in 1295 and began sweeping reforms with Rashid al-Din.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 10, <b>Timurid Empire</b>: &#8220;Timur, produced by the Chagatai realm, conquered from Anatolia to the borders of China, and his heirs made Samarkand and Herat centres of painting, poetry and astronomy.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;Follow Genghis Khan's empire from the steppe to the rival khanates and Timur's heirs.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/the-mongol-world. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: The Mongol World</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Mongol Empire <span>from 1206 CE</span></li><li>Hulagu Khan <span>born c. 1217 CE</span></li><li>Golden Horde <span>from 1242 CE</span></li><li>Ilkhanate <span>from 1256 CE</span></li><li>Yuan Dynasty <span>from 1271 CE</span></li><li>Timurid Empire <span>from 1363 CE</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Match the people to their dates</h2><ol class="ws-key-list" type="A"><li>Batu Khan <span>1: c. 1205–1255</span></li><li>Hulagu Khan <span>3: c. 1217–1265</span></li><li>Kublai Khan <span>2: 1215–1294</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Key terms</h2><dl class="ws-key-terms"><dt>Inflation</dt><dd>Inflation is a sustained rise in the general level of prices, reducing the purchasing power of money.</dd><dt>Silk Road</dt><dd>The Silk Road was the network of overland trade routes linking China with Central Asia, Iran, the Middle East and the Mediterranean.</dd></dl></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">4</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
